WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.819
Hey Wisecrack, Jared in the the very pale
flesh again.

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:10.600
So we watched Netflix’s new Death Note movie
this weekend, and thought it merited some discussion.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.390
And, of course, some spoilers.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:17.500
If you watched our video on the Death Note anime a couple weeks ago, you know that we’re big fans.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.300
The show draws out incredibly nuanced ideas
about the nature of justice

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.600
Justice

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:26.880
and even participates

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.830
in its own kind of philosophical dialogue
throughout the series.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.439
It’s just damn good television.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.439
The movie?

00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:35.400
Well it’s not exactly good cinema.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.070
Ahhhhh!

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:42.399
Now off the bat, I just wanna say that adapting
a 37 episode anime to a single movie is really,

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:43.500
REALLY hard.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.940
If somebody hired me to do that I’d be s***ing
my pants, so we’re not here to grade how

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.800
closely the new film sticks to the anime or
the manga.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.600
It’s an adaptation, and it deserves some
room to do its own thing.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.059
So let’s look at the film on its own terms.

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:01.280
It’s definitely got some good things going
for it, and the thing that I most admire is

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.969
its attempt to preserve some of the thematic
richness of the anime, which is an impressive

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:12.439
goal for a 100-minute movie that already packs
in a lot— action sequences, detective games,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.280
gore, and teen romance.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.680
From Light’s argument that his systematic
cheating is no big deal

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.600
I'm looking at homework here for at least 15 different students.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.444
All right, I get it. I'm in trouble.

00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:26.800
All I'm trying to say is you have the chance to stop the type of people who make things hard for everybody.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.320
You got to see the big picture here.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.939
To his conversation with his dad at the end,
Death Note tries to put some moral ambiguity

00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:37.700
into Light’s character and to gesture to
bigger questions about the nature of good and evil.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:42.899
Given the time constraints, it was smart not
to attempt the massive discourses on justice

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:43.909
that we see in the anime.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:49.560
But the film makes clear that it’s still
interested in ethics, just in a simplified form.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.560
What exactly can I do with this death note?

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.600
Put your name down, and see what happens.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.479
Unfortunately, that’s where the film gets
itself into trouble.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.780
Toward the beginning, Mr. Turner tells Light

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.800
Here, of course, he’s talking about the
role of the Seattle police.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.500
But Light seems to adopt a similar principle.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.890
Using the power of the Death Note, he— like
his anime counterpart— wants to create a

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:20.580
new world where innocent civilians are protected
from the “people who make life miserable.”

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.570
The Death Note is his gun, and that’s the
aim.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.330
Although he gets squeamish about offing some
law enforcement, we never really see Light

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:35.800
compromising the intentions behind that aim
until his David Copperfield reveal at the end of the film.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.990
Instead, they bring in a seductive, power-hungry
girlfriend to play the role of bad cop.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:47.300
For 90% of the movie, Light is unambiguously
the good guy, or at least the kind-of-okay guy.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.220
And that’s a problem.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.200
Kira largely had the support of the people,
including the police, until the death of the FBI team.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.600
And who sent eight federal agents walking
off a building?

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.570
The duplicitous Mia.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.320
Who stole the notebook from Light and wrote
his name in it?

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.480
Bad, bad Mia.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.400
Who killed Watari by not burning the page
with his name on it?

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.900
We’re clearly supposed to blame Mia… even
though Light put him in there in the first place.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.270
Although the film spends next to no time developing
what Light wants out of his new world, he’s

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:24.220
still shockingly quick to abandon it once
someone dies who hasn’t committed a felony

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.000
or stolen someone’s lunch money.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Do you really think people are gonna be on their knees if you can talk to Kira like that?

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.300
We're not killing my dad! Mia, this is over. Will you f*** this? We're not the good guys anymore.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.420
“We aren’t the good guys anymore” sounds a lot more like “people don’t like us”

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.680
than an actual statement about Kira’s morality
of Rule.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.300
And while the third act of the film tries
to play up the difference between Light’s

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.650
reluctance to kill people and Bad Mia’s
bloodthirstiness, Light’s shift seems more

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.860
pragmatic than anything.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.920
Light’s change of perspective isn’t an
indication that there was ever problems with

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.900
his plan for becoming a God, just that the
circumstances have changed and he doesn’t

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.860
want to go to prison but Mia doesn’t really
mind.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:08.260
If a gun is only as good as its aim, it seems
like the film should be asking questions about

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.340
Light’s aim with the Death Note.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.920
But instead of saying “Be careful of aiming
your gun in this direction,” it’s more

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.080
“make sure your crazy girlfriend doesn’t
get ahold of your gun.”

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.400
Give me my f***ing book, okay?!

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.370
Even L’s quick personality flip from “I
don’t kill; I bring people to justice”

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.159
to “I might kill you in the same way you
killed hundreds of people” seems to be totally

00:04:29.159 --> 00:04:31.060
dependent on circumstance.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:35.740
The detective’s strong moral framework,
developed since childhood, gets tossed away

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.539
instantly when his friend goes missing—
but there’s no emphasis on the significance

00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:42.240
of that change.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.000
Light ends the film by telling his dad

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.560
But what are these two evils?

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:03.800
My guess is that not even Light knows.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:09.689
Unlike the anime—I know, sorry, I had to say it—
Death Note’s ethical ambiguity is less about

00:05:09.689 --> 00:05:14.800
opening a multitude of possibilities and more
about shrugging in the direction of good and evil,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.400
while ultimately saying nothing.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.100
(evil laughter and depressing music)

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.500
What?

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:25.100
You humans are so...interesting (evil laughter)

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:31.180
Now, once again, condensing an anime of that magnitude into a relatively short film is a difficult

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.800
task, and I won’t begrudge the filmmakers
some reductions in depth.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.400
But what the story clung to most— questions
about good and evil— was poorly executed

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.490
and ended up hamstringing its potential, both
philosophically and dramatically.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.879
Anyway, those are our thoughts.

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:50.430
We’re still experimenting with this new
quicktake format, so if you have thoughts

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.949
on what we should cover next, let us know.

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:54.160
Peace guys.

